<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Menton</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mentone</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Menton is a town on the French Riviera close to the Italian border, known for its lemons and mild climate. Held by the Grimaldi princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monaco">Monaco</a> from the 14th century, it rebelled in 1848, declared itself a free city with Roquebrune and passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1861 after a plebiscite, Monaco being compensated with four million francs. In the 19th century it became a popular winter resort.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/menton.jpg" alt="Menton" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Le poulpe ambidextre · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Menton_february_13.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Menton lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> at the Franco-Italian border, just across from the Ligurian town of Ventimiglia. It has always been a frontier town: since the end of the 14th century it has stood on the border between the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a>, held by the Duke of Savoy, and the Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>. The mild climate favours groves of lemons, oranges, mandarins and clementines, and the lemon is one of the town's symbols.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first major settlement came in the 11th century, when the count of Ventimiglia built the Château de Puypin. In the 13th century the Vento family of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> built a new castle along the Roman road, around which the present town grew. The first mention of Menton is in the peace treaty between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-anjou">Charles of Anjou</a> and Genoa, dated 21 July 1262. In 1346 Charles Grimaldi, Lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monaco">Monaco</a>, acquired it, and the princes of Monaco ruled it until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a>.</p><p>In 1848, partly because of a tax on lemon exports, Menton and its neighbour Roquebrune seceded from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monaco">Monaco</a> and proclaimed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-imperial-city">Free Cities</a> of Menton and Roquebrune, hoping to join the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>. Two years later they placed themselves under its protection and were administered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-savoy">House of Savoy</a> for ten years.</p><p>The Treaty of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> of 24 March 1860 provided for the annexation of the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nice">Nice</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, subject to a plebiscite. It was held on 15 and 16 April 1860, and Menton and Roquebrune voted 833 to 54 in favour. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon-iii">Napoleon III</a> paid 4 million francs in compensation to the prince of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monaco">Monaco</a>, who renounced his rights on 2 February 1861.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>James Henry Bennett's book Winter and Spring on the Shores of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> (1861) made Menton a destination for sufferers of tuberculosis, and by the end of the 19th century tourism was an important part of its growth. British and Russian aristocrats built many of the hotels, villas and palaces that remain. In June 1940 Menton was the only sizable settlement captured by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in its invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and Italy annexed two thirds of the commune until 8 September 1943. American and Canadian troops of the First Special Service Force liberated it on 8 September 1944. It is nicknamed the Pearl of France and is known for its gardens.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Menton” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Between 1346 and 1355 the Grimaldi annexed <b>Menton</b> and Roquebrune, increasing their territory almost tenfold, and in the late 1300s Genoa lost Monaco while fighting the Crown of Aragon over Corsica.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-monaco">Principality of Monaco · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Menton</b> and Roquebrune formed most of the Principality of Monaco's territory and income, through their lemon and olive trade.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-cities-of-menton-and-roquebrune">Free Cities of Menton and Roquebrune</a></li></ul></section>
